ROWS = [
dict(id='T196',
 x='Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Sample compression conjecture: learning theory and combinatorics.',
 d='STAT',
 t='Linear-size sample compression (Littlestone–Warmuth)',
 s='Does every binary concept class C contained in {0,1}^X of finite VC dimension d admit a labelled sample compression scheme of size O(d)? The compressor must retain at most Cd labelled examples from every finite sample realizable by C, with a universal constant C, and the fixed reconstruction map must recover a hypothesis consistent with the whole sample.',
 c='The size bound is independent of the sample size. The original size-d conjecture is stronger; this row asks the customary linear-size version. Finite compression of size exponential in d is known, so merely obtaining a sample-size-independent bound is not the problem. Regression, multiclass learning and approximate or randomized reconstructions are different formulations.',
 src='J. H. Rubinstein and B. I. P. Rubinstein, Unlabelled Sample Compression Schemes for Intersection-Closed Classes and Extremal Classes, arXiv:2210.05455 (2022); see also Attias, Hanneke and Ramaswami, arXiv:2410.13012',
 loc="Rubinstein–Rubinstein, Introduction, PDF p. 1: 'This conjecture, for size O(d), remains open now more than 35 years later'. The preceding sentence specifies labelled schemes for VC-d classes.",
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.05455',
 why='Compression would explain finite-VC statistical learnability through a uniformly small set of retained observations. It is one of the oldest structural questions behind distribution-free learning and generalisation bounds.',
 st='Rubinstein and Rubinstein obtain unlabelled schemes of size at most 11d for intersection-closed classes and describe earlier size-d schemes for maximum classes. Attias, Hanneke and Ramaswami (arXiv:2410.13012) still use the binary linear-size conjecture as a hypothesis for reductions to other learning settings. The 2026 agnostic squared-loss compression result arXiv:2609.29696 concerns an accuracy-dependent regression guarantee and does not prove this exact binary reconstruction statement. Open.',
 n='Fame: the opened introduction calls it one of the oldest open problems in learning theory and traces it to Littlestone and Warmuth (1986).'),
dict(id='T197',
 x='Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Free group factor isomorphism problem: operator algebras.',
 d='RMT',
 t='The free group factor isomorphism problem (Murray–von Neumann)',
 s='For the free groups F_m and F_n on distinct finite numbers m,n >= 2 of generators, are the tracial von Neumann algebras L(F_m) and L(F_n) isomorphic? In particular, decide whether L(F_2) is isomorphic to L(F_3). Here L(F_n) is generated by the left regular representation on l²(F_n), with its canonical trace.',
 c='These are separable II_1 factors. The nonseparable analogue is a different problem. Equality of first-order theories is weaker than isomorphism. T145 asks equality of two free-entropy constructions, a potential source of invariants, rather than this classification question.',
 src='I. Goldbring and J. Pi, On the First-Order Free Group Factor Alternative, arXiv:2305.08168v2 (2025), Introduction and Theorem 1.1',
 loc="Introduction, PDF p. 1: 'A long-standing question regarding the free group factors'; the following displayed question asks whether L(F_m) is isomorphic to L(F_n) for distinct m,n >= 2.",
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.08168',
 why='Free group factors are the basic operator algebras of free probability, generated by freely independent Haar unitaries. Distinguishing their number of generators is a central motivation for free entropy and its random-matrix models.',
 st='Goldbring and Pi recall the Dykema–Radulescu dichotomy: all interpolated free group factors are isomorphic or all their distinct parameters give nonisomorphic factors. Their new results concern elementary equivalence and do not choose between these alternatives. Boutonnet, Drimbe, Ioana and Popa (arXiv:2308.05671) settle a nonseparable analogue, which does not settle the separable question. A proof that L(F_n) ≅ L(F_2) for every n ≥ 2 is credited to OpenAI ("An isomorphism of the free group factors", 23 September 2026, 23 pages, not on arXiv), and Shlyakhtenko (arXiv:2610.19590) gives a self-contained proof of the stronger statement C*(S_1, …, S_n) ≅ C*(S_1, S_2) for free semicircular systems by the same strategy. Neither is refereed. An explicit isomorphism is not known.',
 n='Fame: the opened source attributes the problem to Murray and von Neumann; it is a standard foundational problem of free probability and II_1-factor theory.'),
dict(id='T198',
 x="Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Falconer's distance set conjecture: harmonic analysis.",
 d='GEOM',
 t="Falconer's distance set conjecture in the plane",
 s='Let E be a compact subset of R² with Hausdorff dimension greater than 1. Must its Euclidean distance set Delta(E) = {|x-y| : x,y in E} have positive one-dimensional Lebesgue measure?',
 c='The threshold 1 is half the ambient dimension. Full Hausdorff dimension of the distance set is a weaker conclusion than positive Lebesgue measure. Pinned distance sets fix one endpoint and pose a stronger question. Finite-field and integer-lattice distance problems are separate variants; none is counted here as another row. T181 concerns projections of a fixed Cantor set rather than its distance set.',
 src="A. Iosevich and A. Yavicoli, Improved dimension bounds for Falconer's distance problem in the plane, arXiv:2610.05500 (2026), Introduction",
 loc="Introduction, PDF p. 5: 'Falconer conjectured that' the distance set 'has positive Lebesgue measure whenever' the source dimension exceeds 1. The next sentence says even the weaker full-dimension conclusion remains open near 1.",
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.05500',
 why='This is a central question about how a nonlinear map transforms fractal probability measures. Frostman measures, Fourier decay, energy integrals and probabilistic constructions are fundamental to both its positive results and its obstructions.',
 st='Iosevich and Yavicoli (October 2026) improve lower bounds for the Hausdorff dimension of distance sets when the source dimension is just above 1. Those bounds do not give positive Lebesgue measure. Gan, Liu and Wu (arXiv:2610.05015) revisit the 5/4 positive-measure result; the full threshold 1 remains out of reach in the opened current literature. Open.',
 n="Fame: a named four-decade conjecture central to geometric measure theory and its interaction with probability; the October 2026 source describes it as one of the field's most intriguing problems."),
dict(id='T199',
 x='Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Capacity region of the broadcast channel: information theory.',
 d='STAT',
 t='The capacity region of the general two-receiver broadcast channel',
 s='Given a finite-alphabet discrete memoryless channel p(y,z|x) with one sender and two receivers, determine the capacity region of achievable rate triples (R_0,R_1,R_2), where the common message is decoded by both receivers and receiver i also decodes its own private message, with error probability tending to zero as block length tends to infinity.',
 c='The target is a general characterization beyond the regularized block-coding definition. Degraded, deterministic and Gaussian subclasses have special solutions. It is no longer appropriate to identify the capacity region with the one-letter Marton inner bound: a 2026 paper reports a counterexample. T166 concerns two interfering senders, a different channel model.',
 src="M. Huang, Y. Liu and Y. Liu, Sub-optimality of Marton's Inner Bound for the Two-Receiver Broadcast Channel, arXiv:2608.19869 (2026), Introduction; original model attributed there to Cover (1972)",
 loc="Introduction, PDF p. 1: 'the full characterization of the capacity region remains one of the most fundamental open problems in information theory for decades'. Figure 1 specifies common and private messages.",
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.19869',
 why="The model isolates how one random channel input can carry different information to different receivers. Its unresolved capacity is a basic obstruction to a general theory of multiuser communication and has motivated random coding, auxiliary-variable and entropy methods since Cover's formulation.",
 st='Huang, Liu and Liu, Theorem 5, construct a finite two-receiver channel for which the complete one-letter Marton region is strictly below capacity, by proving a two-letter gain. This refutes the optimality conjecture for that bound, not the existence of an as-yet unknown capacity characterization. The opened 2026 source still identifies the general capacity problem as open; subsequent searched results concern subclasses or assisted channels. Open.',
 n='Fame: one of the classical unsolved problems of Shannon theory, explicitly described as fundamental in the opened 2026 paper.'),
dict(id="T200", d="BM", t="Exact intersection exponents of three-dimensional Brownian motion",
 s="Determine the three-dimensional Brownian intersection exponents xi_3(k,lambda), for integers k >= 1 and real lambda > 0. In the standard definition, k independent Brownian paths start at 0 and another starts at (1,0,0); Z_n is the conditional probability that the latter path avoids the first k until all have reached the sphere of radius exp(n), given the first k paths. Then xi_3(k,lambda) = -lim n^(-1) log E[Z_n^lambda]. In particular, determine xi_3(1,1).",
 c="This is a question about exact values, not existence or analyticity of the exponents. The special value xi_3(1,2)=1 is known. T103 concerns loop-erased random walk and its growth exponent; Brownian intersection exponents describe avoidance of independent un-erased paths and a whole moment family.",
 src="Y. Gao, X. Li, Y. Li, R. Liu and X. Liu, A boundary Harnack principle and its application to analyticity of 3D Brownian intersection exponents, arXiv:2411.14921 (2024), Section 1.1",
 loc="Section 1.1, PDF p. 3: 'Although determining all values of' xi_3(k,lambda) 'is still out of reach'. Equation (1.1), PDF p. 2, defines the exponents.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.14921",
 why="These exponents govern nonintersection probabilities and the fractal geometry of Brownian cut points. Their planar analogues were landmark applications of SLE; three dimensions lack the conformal tools that produced those exact answers.",
 st="Gao and collaborators prove real analyticity in lambda for every positive integer k (Theorem 1.1); this does not identify the values. Liu (arXiv:2609.18643) proves the explicit bound xi_3(1,1)<0.9999. Shi, Liu, Li, Li and Deng (arXiv:2609.25968) give numerical estimates for several packet sizes and continuous moments, explicitly describing them as numerical. No general exact expression is known. Open.",
 n="Fame: a classical Brownian critical-exponent problem, treated in Lawler's work and in the literature surrounding the Lawler–Schramm–Werner planar intersection-exponent theorems."),
dict(id="T201", d="IPS", t="A nontrivial traffic transition in the Biham–Middleton–Levine model",
 s="Initially each site of Z² independently contains a northbound car with probability p/2, an eastbound car with probability p/2, or a vacancy with probability 1-p. At odd steps all northbound cars with an empty site immediately north move simultaneously, and at even steps eastbound cars do likewise eastward. Is there p_c in (0,1) such that for p>p_c almost surely no car moves infinitely often, whereas for p<p_c almost surely every car moves infinitely often?",
 c="This is the infinite-lattice conjecture, with positive-density independent initial data. A theorem for finitely many cars, or for a vanishing density on finite tori, does not establish its low-density phase. The stronger assertion of asymptotic free-flow speed 1/2 is discussed separately in the posing paper. Intermediate periodic states on finite tori need not contradict the infinite-volume statement.",
 src="O. Angel, A. E. Holroyd and J. B. Martin, The Jammed Phase of the Biham–Middleton–Levine Traffic Model, arXiv:math/0504001 (2005)",
 loc="PDF p. 3, unnumbered Conjecture: 'There exists' a critical density in (0,1); below it 'almost surely all cars move infinitely often'. The same paragraph states the jammed conclusion above it.",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0504001",
 why="This is a standard example of collective self-organisation arising from deterministic dynamics with random initial conditions. Even proving flow at a fixed sufficiently small positive density would require overcoming persistent long-range blocking dependencies.",
 st="Angel, Holroyd and Martin prove that sufficiently high density gives jamming, on Z² and on large tori of bounded aspect ratio. Rabinovich, Biham and Katzav (arXiv:2601.01180v2, 2026) still describe only a few rigorous results and study low-density torus dynamics through geometric observables and simulations. Their analysis does not prove the infinite-lattice conjecture at positive low density. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline: the BML model is a widely studied foundational traffic cellular automaton; the posing paper notes its appearance as an unsolved puzzle."),
dict(id='T202',
 x="Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Mahler's conjecture: convex geometry.",
 d='GEOM',
 t="Mahler's minimum volume-product conjecture in dimension four and higher",
 s='For every convex body K in R^n, n>=4, let P(K)=min over z in the interior of K of vol_n(K) vol_n((K-z)^polar), where A^polar={y: <x,y><=1 for every x in A}. Prove P(K)>=(n+1)^(n+1)/(n!)², the value for a simplex, with equality only for simplices.',
 c='This is the general, nonsymmetric conjecture. The centrally symmetric version predicts the different minimum 4^n/n!, attained by Hanner polytopes. Both belong to one volume-product programme and are not counted as separate rows. The three-dimensional general case now has a reported solution and is excluded from the open range.',
 src='M. Fradelizi, M. Meyer and A. Zvavitch, Volume Product, arXiv:2301.06131 (2023), Section 3, Conjecture 1',
 loc="Section 3, PDF p. 15, Conjecture 1: 'For every convex body K in' R^n; the displayed inequality gives the simplex value, 'with equality if and only if' K is a simplex.",
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.06131',
 why='The volume product measures a convex body together with its dual. It connects log-concave probability, entropy and transport inequalities with asymptotic convex geometry. Sharp lower bounds would determine the extremal geometry behind inverse Santalo inequalities.',
 st='The 2023 survey records the planar general case and the three-dimensional symmetric case. Chen, Li, Xi and Xu (arXiv:2605.09334v3) report a proof for all three-dimensional convex bodies, including the equality case, using shadow flow. This does not cover n>=4. The current search also found special-symmetry and functional results rather than a general higher-dimensional solution. Open.',
 n='Fame: a classical named conjecture dating to 1938, central to convex geometry and routinely connected to log-concave probability.'),
dict(id='T203',
 x='Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Shannon capacity of the seven-cycle: combinatorics and information theory.',
 d='STAT',
 t='The Shannon capacity of the seven-cycle',
 s='Determine Theta(C_7)=lim as k tends to infinity of alpha(C_7 strong-product ... strong-product C_7)^(1/k), where the product has k factors and alpha denotes its independence number. Equivalently, determine the optimal zero-error communication rate log_2 Theta(C_7) for a channel whose confusability graph is the seven-cycle.',
 c='The strong product joins two distinct tuples when every coordinate agrees or is adjacent in C_7. The five-cycle has capacity sqrt(5), but the analogous exact value for the seven-cycle remains unknown. Finite-product independence numbers and improved lower bounds do not identify the limit. T117 concerns a stochastic deletion channel rather than zero-error capacity of a fixed confusability graph.',
 src='N. Lavi and I. Sason, Advances in the Shannon Capacity of Graphs, arXiv:2509.24600v3 (2026); Y. Gao, A Recursive Construction Improving the Lower Bound on the Shannon Capacity of C7, arXiv:2607.27869 (2026)',
 loc="Lavi–Sason, Example 4.1, PDF p. 26, equation (120), displays a strict interval for Theta(C_7). Gao, abstract, PDF p. 1: 'We give a recursive reformulation and extension' of a large independent set in a power of C_7.",
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.24600',
 why='The smallest unresolved odd cycle is a landmark test case for zero-error information theory. Its capacity captures the advantage of long block codes over a single use and has driven semidefinite bounds and probabilistic-combinatorial constructions.',
 st="The Lovasz theta upper bound is 7 cos(pi/7)/(1+cos(pi/7)), about 3.31767. The opened January 2026 survey gives a strict lower/upper gap; July 2026 papers improve lower bounds using larger independent sets and recursive constructions, including Gao's explicit set in C_7^200. Those constructions leave a gap to the upper bound and do not determine capacity. Open.",
 n="Fame: the canonical next case after Lovasz's celebrated solution for C_5; a decades-old problem in zero-error information theory and probabilistic combinatorics.")
]
